<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Berg</h1><p class="st-meta">1101 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Düsseldorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhineland duchy east of the Rhine with its capital at Düsseldorf, which became part of the Jülich lands and later a Napoleonic grand duchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count Adolf of Berg appears in 1101 with a castle on the Dhün river; the family later built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schloss-burg">Burg Castle</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wupper">Wupper</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-ii-of-berg">Engelbert I of Berg</a>, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>, was murdered in 1225. In 1288 Count Adolf V helped defeat the archbishop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> and granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> town rights.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Berg was raised to a duchy in 1380. In 1423 it was united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-julich">Duchy of Jülich</a> and in 1521 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>. Its industry developed early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wupper">Wupper</a> valley and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solingen">Solingen</a>, known for blades. Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-william-elector-palatine">Johann Wilhelm</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in the early 18th century, building it into a court city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1614 Berg and Jülich belonged to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made Berg a grand duchy for his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a>. In 1815 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> is now capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-berg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Berg" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: ZH2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Berg_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1101 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Düsseldorf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolf V of Berg, Johann Wilhelm of the Palatinate, Joachim Murat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, with Protestant minorities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low Rhenish and Ripuarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Berg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=duchy-of-berg" aria-label="Duchy of Berg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4834 403 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Berg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5154 659L5259 732M5154 659L5229 694M5154 659L5154 659M5154 659L5154 659M5154 659L5193 634"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 732h0M5229 694h0M5154 659h0M5154 659h0M5193 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5154 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Düsseldorf. <a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=duchy-of-berg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
